            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "This should now be (finally) resolved on latest `main`.\n\nThe solution ended up to be not quite \"... plug a function somewhere so that the source used by the buffer can be programmatically decided\". Instead it is now way easier to do a common case of setting up several possible sources to be attempted to attach: [just set an array of sources](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/2e38ed16c2ced64bcd576986ccad4b18e2006e18/doc/mini-diff.txt#L337-L339) (here is a [concrete example](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/2e38ed16c2ced64bcd576986ccad4b18e2006e18/doc/mini-diff.txt#L535-L536)).\n\nSo the case of \"attach either Git or Mercurial source\" is something like:\n\n```lua\nlocal mercurial_source = {\n  name = 'mercurial',\n  -- IMPORTANT: make sure to call `MiniDiff.fail_attach()` if can't attach to a particular file\n  attach = function(buf_id) ... end,\n  detach = function(buf_id) ... end,\n  apply_hunks = function(buf_id, hunks) ... end,\n}\n\nlocal diff = require('mini.diff')\ndiff.setup({ source = { diff.gen_source.git(), mercurial_source } })\n```\n\nThis will for each file to first try to attach Git source and if it fails (i.e. the file is not tracked by Git; even not immediately) - try attaching Mercurial.\n\nFor a more complex logic of deciding which source should be attach the suggestion is indeed to wrap them in a custom source:\n- Decide in `attach` which \"subsource\" is active. Cache its index/table.\n- Use cached index/table inside \"supersource\" `detach` / `apply_hunks`.",
